WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.735
Back at the end of the bakery line, the packer has a small table where loaves wait to be boxed.

2
00:00:08.735 --> 00:00:09.560
It holds ten.

3
00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:17.056
As long as the oven waits for her slips, the table never overflows: she writes send me 10 more only when there's room.

4
00:00:17.533 --> 00:00:20.297
But not everything in a bakery reads slips.

5
00:00:20.297 --> 00:00:27.945
The oven's thermometer is a chatterbox: it reports the temperature every millisecond, whether anyone is looking or not.

6
00:00:27.945 --> 00:00:32.444
Nobody ever asked it to slow down, and it wouldn't listen if they did.

7
00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:39.825
Its readings go to a screen in the shop, which asks for one at a time and takes 10 milliseconds to draw each.

8
00:00:39.825 --> 00:00:47.443
In Reactor, Flux.interval plays the thermometer: it sends a tick, a number, every millisecond, whatever you request.

9
00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:49.954
Reading 0 is asked for, and drawn.

10
00:00:49.954 --> 00:00:57.621
Reading 1 arrives before anyone asked for it, and the stream fails: could not emit tick 1 due to lack of requests.

11
00:00:58.333 --> 00:01:03.118
Reactor has a family of operators for this, with backpressure in their names.

12
00:01:03.118 --> 00:01:12.875
Here's the question that's easy to be shy about: backpressure means the slow side slows down the fast side, so surely one of them slows the thermometer down?

13
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.862
Let's try the one everyone reaches for first: onBackpressureBuffer, with no size.

14
00:01:18.862 --> 00:01:21.006
The screen takes twenty readings.

15
00:01:21.006 --> 00:01:22.111
What does it get?

16
00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.996
Readings 0, 1, 2, all the way to 19.

17
00:01:27.996 --> 00:01:29.846
In order, and no error.

18
00:01:29.846 --> 00:01:31.214
It looks perfect.

19
00:01:31.214 --> 00:01:32.743
That's the problem.

20
00:01:32.967 --> 00:01:37.435
When the screen drew reading 19, the thermometer was at 216.

21
00:01:37.435 --> 00:01:44.585
Nearly 200 readings were waiting in a queue with no limit, about 90 more every 100 milliseconds.

22
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:48.587
It's our doorstep with fourteen loaves, back again.

23
00:01:48.587 --> 00:01:54.024
We fixed the smoke alarm by taking out the battery: the noise stopped, the fire didn't.

24
00:01:54.024 --> 00:01:56.086
No error doesn't mean no problem.

25
00:01:56.086 --> 00:01:59.336
The pile just moved into memory, where nobody looks.

26
00:02:00.167 --> 00:02:02.356
So, back to the packer's table.

27
00:02:02.356 --> 00:02:09.064
When a belt won't stop, the table fills, and someone must decide what happens to the next item.

28
00:02:09.064 --> 00:02:10.476
Four honest answers.

29
00:02:10.476 --> 00:02:11.747
Keep a small pile.

30
00:02:11.747 --> 00:02:13.018
Throw it in a bin.

31
00:02:13.018 --> 00:02:14.501
Keep only the newest.

32
00:02:14.501 --> 00:02:15.701
Or stop the line.

33
00:02:15.933 --> 00:02:19.254
Each onBackpressure operator is one of those answers.

34
00:02:19.254 --> 00:02:28.149
It asks the thermometer for everything, so nothing overflows, and then decides what happens to the readings nobody further down has asked for.

35
00:02:28.967 --> 00:02:32.724
The small pile is onBackpressureBuffer with a size, say 10.

36
00:02:32.724 --> 00:02:36.036
While the screen draws reading 0, the pile fills up.

37
00:02:36.036 --> 00:02:42.277
Then a reading finds no room, so the line stops: the thermometer is cut off, and an error is sent.

38
00:02:42.267 --> 00:02:45.068
Does the screen still get the readings on the pile?

39
00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:48.645
It does.

40
00:02:48.645 --> 00:02:56.163
The error waits its turn behind the pile: readings 0 to 11, in order, and then, the receiver is overrun.

41
00:02:56.163 --> 00:02:58.476
Nothing vanished without a word.

42
00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.538
The bin is onBackpressureDrop.

43
00:03:01.538 --> 00:03:10.863
A reading that arrives while the screen isn't asking goes straight in: the screen saw 0, 11, 22, 34, and 202 went in the bin.

44
00:03:10.863 --> 00:03:11.833
Why 11 apart?

45
00:03:11.833 --> 00:03:17.801
When the screen asks, the bin has nothing on hand, so it waits for the next one.

46
00:03:18.633 --> 00:03:21.663
Keep only the newest is onBackpressureLatest.

47
00:03:21.663 --> 00:03:25.973
It has one slot, and each new reading pushes out the one before.

48
00:03:25.973 --> 00:03:31.091
When the screen asks, the newest is ready: 0, 10, 20, 30, about every tenth.

49
00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:37.451
And stop the line is onBackpressureError: it fails at the first reading nobody asked for.

50
00:03:37.451 --> 00:03:40.383
That's for belts where falling behind is a bug.

51
00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:44.965
So, does any of them slow the thermometer down?

52
00:03:44.965 --> 00:03:45.487
Not one.

53
00:03:45.487 --> 00:03:50.704
Honest names would be: keep a pile, use the bin, keep the newest, stop the line.

54
00:03:50.704 --> 00:03:52.139
They're loss policies.

55
00:03:52.139 --> 00:03:55.139
They decide what to keep, and what to give up.

56
00:03:55.967 --> 00:03:58.056
Here the bakery picture breaks.

57
00:03:58.056 --> 00:04:00.010
A full table is hard to miss.

58
00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:08.637
A buffer with no size is a table nobody can see, and it grows until the heap, the memory where Java keeps its objects, runs out.

59
00:04:09.467 --> 00:04:19.060
Real backpressure needs a source that reads the slips, like the database cursor behind the nightly report, which fetches only the rows it's asked for.

60
00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:21.811
That's backpressure for free, so prefer it.

61
00:04:22.633 --> 00:04:25.588
When you can't, ask what one missed item costs.

62
00:04:25.588 --> 00:04:27.852
A temperature: newer replaces older.

63
00:04:27.852 --> 00:04:33.071
A buffer here would show the oven's temperature from a minute ago, very accurately.

64
00:04:33.071 --> 00:04:34.265
So keep the newest.

65
00:04:34.467 --> 00:04:39.734
A visitor count, one sample of hundreds, can go in the bin, if you count the drops.

66
00:04:39.734 --> 00:04:48.112
A web order must never vanish: give it a small pile that fails loudly, and a way to ask the website again from the last saved order.

67
00:04:48.933 --> 00:04:58.711
Here it is in Java and Kotlin: the screen keeps the newest reading, and web orders get a pile of 1,000 that logs the order that overflows.

68
00:04:59.533 --> 00:05:02.484
So, does onBackpressure slow the source down?

69
00:05:02.484 --> 00:05:02.680
No.

70
00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:05.696
Only a source that reads the slips slows down.

71
00:05:05.696 --> 00:05:13.171
For one that can't, choose what to lose when the table is full: a small pile, a bin, the newest, or stop the line.

72
00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:14.214
Quick check.

73
00:05:14.214 --> 00:05:17.468
A screen in the shop shows the oven temperature.

74
00:05:17.468 --> 00:05:23.300
The thermometer sends 200 readings a second, and the screen redraws 10 times a second.

75
00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:24.520
Which policy fits?

76
00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:29.862
Keep the newest: onBackpressureLatest.

77
00:05:29.862 --> 00:05:35.023
At every redraw, the screen wants the temperature now, not an old one from a queue.
